From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 00:37:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --callee-regs writes back and reloads around a call
 only the registers the callee touches

With --reg-locals a caller writes back every register it changed before a
call and loads every one it uses after it, since the callee may read or
change any of them. touched.py finds, for each generated function, the
register globals it and everything it calls by name may read or write (a
function that calls anything else -- an indirect call, a call by address,
hand-written code, port I/O, a trace hook -- may touch them all). Around a
plain direct call to a function that leaves the x87 stack alone, x87_locals
now writes back and reloads only those (esp always); the caller's other
locals, its x87 stack positions included, stay the truth across the call,
with no store before it and no load after it.

A safe point inside the callee may run other guest code (an APC); that
code gives back the registers of the code it interrupted, as it must where
the registers are in locals already.
